//! `OpenRTB` 2.6 and 3.0, `AdCOM` 1.0 and Native 1.2 types: a port of the Go module
//! `github.com/prebid/openrtb/v20` at v20.3.0 (packages `openrtb2`, `openrtb3`, `adcom1`,
//! `native1`, `native1/request` and `native1/response`), the model layer of Prebid Server.
//!
//! Every exported Go type, field and typed constant is here, generated by `pbs-devtools typegen`
//! from what go/types reports about the Go packages (see `typegen.json`): a Go struct is a Rust
//! struct with the same fields in the same order, a Go named type is a single-field tuple struct
//! (`adcom1::PlacementPosition(pub i8)`), and a constant keeps its exact value under a
//! `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` name (`adcom1.PositionAboveFold` is
//! [`adcom1::POSITION_ABOVE_FOLD`]). Field types follow Go exactly: integer widths (`int8` is
//! `i8`, `int` is `isize`), pointers as `Option` (`Option<Box<T>>` for structs), slices as
//! `Option<Vec<T>>` and maps as `Option<BTreeMap<K, V>>` (Go tells a nil slice from an empty
//! one, and so does prebid-server), and `json.RawMessage` as [`pbs_gojson::RawJson`]. Field
//! names are the JSON keys (`bidfloorcur`, `gpp_sid`, `r#type`), and each field's doc is its Go
//! doc comment.
//!
//! The types carry `#[derive(GoJson)]` with their Go names and `json` tags, so pbs-gojson
//! encodes and decodes them exactly as Go's `encoding/json` and prebid-server's `jsonutil`
//! (jsoniter) do, byte for byte and error for error:
//!
//! ```
//! use pbs_gojson::jsonutil;
//! use pbs_openrtb::{adcom1, openrtb2};
//!
//! let mut imp = openrtb2::Imp::default();
//! jsonutil::unmarshal(br#"{"id":"1","banner":{"w":300,"pos":1}}"#, &mut imp)?;
//! let banner = imp.banner.as_deref().ok_or("no banner")?;
//! assert_eq!(banner.pos, Some(adcom1::POSITION_ABOVE_FOLD));
//! assert_eq!(banner.w, Some(300));
//! assert_eq!(
//!     jsonutil::marshal(&imp)?,
//!     br#"{"id":"1","banner":{"w":300,"pos":1}}"#
//! );
//! # Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
//! ```
//!
//! The Go functions and methods are ported by hand next to the generated code:
//! [`openrtb2::int8_ptr`], [`openrtb2::int64_ptr`], [`native1::response::int64_ptr`], and the
//! `Ptr`/`Val` methods of [`openrtb3::NoBidReason`] and of the `adcom1` types that have them.
//!
//! # Differences from Go
//!
//! * Go strings are `String`s, which cannot hold invalid UTF-8. jsoniter keeps an invalid byte of a
//!   JSON string in the Go string, and both encoders then write it as an escaped U+FFFD; the
//!   `String` holds U+FFFD itself, which is written as its UTF-8 bytes: the same JSON string, but
//!   different bytes. (`encoding/json` replaces invalid UTF-8 while decoding, so after it the two
//!   agree.) Regenerating with `"strings": "GoString"` in `typegen.json` would make the bytes match
//!   at the cost of byte-string fields.
//! * Six untyped Go constants (`openrtb3.SecondPricePlus` and `DealPrice`, the four
//!   `native1.PlacementType*`) have the named type they are used as.
//!
//! The parity tests (`tests/parity`) replay the goldens of `testdata/go` and the openrtb module's
//! own tests; see their crate docs.

pub mod adcom1;
pub mod native1;
pub mod openrtb2;
pub mod openrtb3;
